1
00:00:10,176 --> 00:00:18,298
[SPEAKER_01]: I was wondering if we could speak about lying, the phenomenon of lying, the choice to lie.

2
00:00:19,578 --> 00:00:23,600
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a simple question, is it wrong to lie?

3
00:00:26,780 --> 00:00:29,961
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, where can we go with that?

4
00:00:29,981 --> 00:00:30,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure.

5
00:00:32,342 --> 00:00:34,182
[SPEAKER_00]: It is.

6
00:00:34,202 --> 00:00:36,783
[SPEAKER_00]: So now you can ask, why is it wrong to lie?

7
00:00:39,443 --> 00:00:49,169
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, don't just take it as an authority who declared that lying is wrong and now you must obey by it.

8
00:00:49,810 --> 00:00:54,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, lying is wrong, but you want to investigate why it is wrong.

9
00:00:54,693 --> 00:01:04,359
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what's the first, most obvious thing that shows itself if you start to investigate why lying would be wrong?

10
00:01:04,780 --> 00:01:05,420
[SPEAKER_00]: What is lying?

11
00:01:06,508 --> 00:01:07,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Dishonesty.

12
00:01:07,709 --> 00:01:08,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Dishonesty.

13
00:01:08,210 --> 00:01:14,675
[SPEAKER_00]: But, okay, so what are, like, dishonesty, lying, concealing, what all those things have in common?

14
00:01:17,177 --> 00:01:18,939
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not wanting something to be known.

15
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:20,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

16
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:22,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, something is, but you want to be different.

17
00:01:22,422 --> 00:01:26,666
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in its nature, it's a... it's a... it's a... it's a discrepancy.

18
00:01:26,686 --> 00:01:29,848
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a fundamental... it's a contradiction.

19
00:01:29,908 --> 00:01:30,949
[SPEAKER_00]: The two opposing things.

20
00:01:31,209 --> 00:01:31,790
[SPEAKER_00]: One is the truth.

21
00:01:32,511 --> 00:01:33,992
[SPEAKER_00]: The other one is what you would prefer to be.

22
00:01:35,763 --> 00:01:58,918
[SPEAKER_00]: But the problem with lying is, phenomenologically speaking, from a point... from a point of view of a person who practices mindfulness, trance, level of wisdom, the problem of lying is that you can't... you cannot deny the truth without implying that... without giving importance to your point of view over the truth.

23
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:03,281
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why lying is practically wrong.

24
00:02:03,441 --> 00:02:05,022
[SPEAKER_00]: Lying is practically an obstacle.

25
00:02:06,465 --> 00:02:20,712
[SPEAKER_00]: If you could say, theoretically, lie without perverting that existential order of truth and my point of view, then lying would not be unwholesome.

26
00:02:21,712 --> 00:02:35,498
[SPEAKER_00]: But it is impossible to engage in an act of lying without implicitly, immediately, eh... fueling the wrong order whereby

27
00:02:36,050 --> 00:02:40,394
[SPEAKER_00]: It's my point of view, my preference, that comes first.

28
00:02:40,714 --> 00:02:41,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Truth is second.

29
00:02:42,676 --> 00:02:47,120
[SPEAKER_00]: And when I say truth, I mean factual presence of a self-realism phenomena.

30
00:02:48,061 --> 00:02:48,982
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the key here.

31
00:02:49,442 --> 00:02:50,383
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like the truth.

32
00:02:51,705 --> 00:02:53,006
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a lie and then there is a truth.

33
00:02:53,066 --> 00:02:53,206
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

34
00:02:54,267 --> 00:03:00,693
[SPEAKER_00]: You denying, well, you denying that whatever the truth is at the time, by serving a lie,

35
00:03:01,193 --> 00:03:11,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Means you implicitly also overriding the the most self-evident basic nature of the arisen experience, that it comes first, structurally first.

36
00:03:13,055 --> 00:03:14,816
[SPEAKER_00]: Your point of view is structurally second.

37
00:03:17,176 --> 00:03:22,157
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by engaging in a lie, you're engaging in perversion of that order.

38
00:03:23,617 --> 00:03:27,558
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why even a little lie is as bad as a bad lie, as a big lie.

39
00:03:28,227 --> 00:03:35,151
[SPEAKER_00]: From the point of view of the mind that wants to practice, because practice means undoing any perversion to the existential order of the five aggregates.

40
00:03:39,033 --> 00:03:47,918
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, like, you cannot engage in a sensual act without sensual perceptions being implied.

41
00:03:48,898 --> 00:03:51,160
[SPEAKER_00]: And sensual perceptions are sensual distortions.

42
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:55,182
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, you can't engage in sensuality without having

43
00:03:56,383 --> 00:04:04,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Without being distorted by sensuality at the same time, without fueling already persisting distortion of central perception.

44
00:04:05,585 --> 00:04:17,707
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't engage in a lie without fueling the existential distortion, basically, of the arisen phenomenon, the truth, the self-evident arising of whatever is present.

45
00:04:19,447 --> 00:04:23,208
[SPEAKER_00]: The arisen experience is there on its own.

46
00:04:25,059 --> 00:04:32,822
[SPEAKER_00]: Means my attitude, my actions upon it, are only because this thing allows my actions to be a possibility.

47
00:04:35,163 --> 00:04:45,546
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, if there is no, say, unpleasant feeling manifested, would you be even able to conceive acting out of an unpleasant feeling, to try to get rid of it?

48
00:04:45,606 --> 00:04:46,067
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't.

49
00:04:47,527 --> 00:04:52,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Or on a less fundamental level, if there's something like, I don't know, take some experience,

50
00:04:54,182 --> 00:04:55,342
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to go to work.

51
00:04:57,383 --> 00:05:10,369
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your reason situation that persists there, that has its significance, like things you have to do, reasons why you have to do it, because you need to earn money, you need to do this, you need to provide.

52
00:05:10,829 --> 00:05:12,409
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's your factual situation.

53
00:05:13,610 --> 00:05:16,711
[SPEAKER_00]: On account of it, you'll be acting left and right, and so on, and deciding.

54
00:05:18,352 --> 00:05:21,453
[SPEAKER_00]: But when you start engaging in an act of lying,

55
00:05:22,954 --> 00:05:24,515
[SPEAKER_00]: You're overriding that very order.

56
00:05:25,235 --> 00:05:26,676
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't mean you can change the order.

57
00:05:27,096 --> 00:05:43,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, there's still things that risen on their own, but you are fully gratuitously giving priority to your point of view, that in itself, at that time, is based on that simply on ignoring what the real order is.

58
00:05:44,867 --> 00:05:47,688
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, you start lying about having to go to work.

59
00:05:47,728 --> 00:05:50,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Lying to yourself, not even lying to others.

60
00:05:53,788 --> 00:05:57,771
[SPEAKER_00]: You start saying, no, you don't need to go to work, because really you don't want to go to work.

61
00:06:01,293 --> 00:06:09,438
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start lying, because the reason thing is unbearable, or not necessarily unbearable, but it's unpleasant and you don't want to have to deal with it.

62
00:06:10,219 --> 00:06:17,743
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why another aspect of an act of lying is, it's always rooted in avoiding displeasure.

63
00:06:18,984 --> 00:06:20,145
[SPEAKER_00]: Why else would you be lying?

64
00:06:21,702 --> 00:06:31,568
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless you yourself perceive that if you don't admit the truth, you will avoid that displeasure, the pain of that truth.

65
00:06:33,910 --> 00:06:35,611
[SPEAKER_00]: But can you really avoid the pain?

66
00:06:38,132 --> 00:06:39,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Through sort of looking elsewhere?

67
00:06:39,433 --> 00:06:39,773
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

68
00:06:39,813 --> 00:06:41,154
[SPEAKER_00]: You're only acting out of it.

69
00:06:41,916 --> 00:06:43,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you're looking elsewhere because it's there.

70
00:06:43,798 --> 00:06:45,059
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly.

71
00:06:45,519 --> 00:06:58,310
[SPEAKER_00]: But, see, the fact that you engaged in an act of lie means you do have, you have, you, well, you had it before and now you're maintaining the belief that you can avoid the arisen pressure of the truth.

72
00:06:59,451 --> 00:07:01,833
[SPEAKER_00]: That this pleasure of the truth simply by lying.

73
00:07:02,994 --> 00:07:09,159
[SPEAKER_00]: Which then means, implies, that you can avoid existentially arisen phenomena simply by providing another phenomena.

74
00:07:11,804 --> 00:07:15,107
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's fundamentally rooted... act of lying is fundamentally rooted in a wrong view.

75
00:07:15,147 --> 00:07:16,588
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's fundamentally unwholesome.

76
00:07:17,388 --> 00:07:20,191
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's the same with any of the precepts that the Buddha laid out.

77
00:07:20,791 --> 00:07:24,554
[SPEAKER_00]: None of them are, like, universally given commandments.

78
00:07:25,235 --> 00:07:40,887
[SPEAKER_00]: All of them are rooted in the principle of greed, aversion, delusion, which, separately, individually, are all also principles of some form of contradicting the arisen nature of experience, which is why it's unwholesome.

79
00:07:42,153 --> 00:07:46,256
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not unwholesome because it's a... it's a universal morality applied to it.

80
00:07:46,276 --> 00:07:50,478
[SPEAKER_00]: They're unwholesome because they go against how things have arisen.

81
00:07:50,958 --> 00:07:57,022
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you can know for yourself that they're unwholesome, once you understand the nature of things, how they have arisen.

82
00:07:59,384 --> 00:08:04,407
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you have a criteria for what's... what's skillful, what's unskillful, what's wholesome, what's unwholesome.

83
00:08:07,849 --> 00:08:09,570
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why lying is always wrong.

84
00:08:10,205 --> 00:08:15,367
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it always goes against the principle, against the nature of an arisen thing, whatever that thing is.

85
00:08:15,827 --> 00:08:22,010
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the example we gave, eh... you don't want to go and do this particular work, now you can admit that.

86
00:08:22,810 --> 00:08:29,372
[SPEAKER_00]: You can... you don't have to lie to yourself, but that means now you have to take responsibility for the... this pleasure you're feeling.

87
00:08:30,993 --> 00:08:36,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, the work needs to be done, but I choose not to, which means all the reasons they were making me do this work,

88
00:08:36,756 --> 00:08:46,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Supporting my family, earning, whatever else necessary, whatever else was the motivation for working, I'm now responsible for not meeting any of those requirements.

89
00:08:48,984 --> 00:08:59,451
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when down the line circumstances remind me of that, my family starts asking for money, or I get sick and I can't pay for my treatment and so on,

90
00:09:00,695 --> 00:09:03,496
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because I chose to not do this particular work.

91
00:09:04,216 --> 00:09:05,436
[SPEAKER_00]: So I'm responsible for that.

92
00:09:05,796 --> 00:09:13,778
[SPEAKER_00]: So now, this future misfortune that came as a result of not being able to look after yourself, is because you chose not to look after yourself.

93
00:09:15,218 --> 00:09:16,238
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing wrong with that.

94
00:09:17,378 --> 00:09:18,498
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing.

95
00:09:18,538 --> 00:09:19,939
[SPEAKER_00]: You have every right to choose to know.

96
00:09:20,759 --> 00:09:24,180
[SPEAKER_00]: But that means you must take responsibility for what comes out of it.

97
00:09:25,380 --> 00:09:26,380
[SPEAKER_00]: But now that means, oh,

98
00:09:27,675 --> 00:09:36,258
[SPEAKER_00]: By trying to avoid the responsibility of doing my duty, for example, now I'm actually paying the price, which is even worse.

99
00:09:37,218 --> 00:09:42,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Now I can't provide for the family, I don't have enough money for this, I don't have enough money for that, I don't have enough money for food, and da-da-da-da-da-da.

100
00:09:43,240 --> 00:09:46,541
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's on you.

101
00:09:46,561 --> 00:09:48,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Result of your choice to avoid work.

102
00:09:49,122 --> 00:09:50,083
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's very unpleasant.

103
00:09:51,343 --> 00:09:52,503
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to admit that it's on you.

104
00:09:52,523 --> 00:09:55,324
[SPEAKER_00]: So now all this... and it can endure for weeks and months, for what?

105
00:09:55,364 --> 00:09:56,925
[SPEAKER_00]: For as long as you don't get out of that situation.

106
00:09:58,017 --> 00:09:59,557
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's still a responsible thing to do.

107
00:10:01,238 --> 00:10:02,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Admit the weight of your choices.

108
00:10:04,119 --> 00:10:08,140
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's way too easy to not admit it.

109
00:10:09,400 --> 00:10:18,463
[SPEAKER_00]: Only, if only, you find another reason for you not doing the work, apart from you not wanting to do the work.

110
00:10:21,084 --> 00:10:25,105
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the nature of bad faith and existential philosophy, which is like

111
00:10:25,728 --> 00:10:34,170
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like lying, but not quite lying, because you're not necessarily fully self-aware of the truth, because it's the very truth you're trying to cover up for yourself.

112
00:10:35,710 --> 00:10:47,093
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now, if I say, well, I couldn't work because of such and such... whatever... things came... like, I couldn't work because it was raining.

113
00:10:49,154 --> 00:10:55,035
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, oh, I couldn't work because I chose rain to be the reason for my not working.

114
00:10:59,070 --> 00:11:05,136
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you say, you know, it was raining, so I couldn't work, which means now, when I don't have enough money, it's because it's the weather's fault.

115
00:11:06,457 --> 00:11:17,307
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you bypass responsibility for not working at the time, and then, consequently, for all the future issues that came out, that there were results of not working, are also bypassed.

116
00:11:17,487 --> 00:11:18,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, see, it's not on me.

117
00:11:19,389 --> 00:11:25,134
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people always, inherently, it's the easiest thing to blame another, or to blame circumstances.

118
00:11:26,883 --> 00:11:30,867
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that inauthentic attitude we speak about often in in our videos.

119
00:11:32,209 --> 00:11:38,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Because unless you take responsibility on that fundamental level, you'll never have a basis for dhamma to apply.

120
00:11:39,316 --> 00:11:41,218
[SPEAKER_00]: And fundamental responsibility is suffering.

121
00:11:42,079 --> 00:11:43,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, like, you don't want to work because it's unpleasant.

122
00:11:44,121 --> 00:11:49,126
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to accept future responsibility for the consequences of your not working because it's unpleasant.

123
00:11:50,323 --> 00:11:57,867
[SPEAKER_00]: So, fundamentally, you don't want to suffer and you keep avoiding displeasure as the only mean of dealing with suffering that you know.

124
00:11:59,468 --> 00:12:00,388
[SPEAKER_00]: But it doesn't deal with it.

125
00:12:02,089 --> 00:12:04,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Evading suffering can only be done because suffering has arisen.

126
00:12:04,931 --> 00:12:05,591
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the truth.

127
00:12:06,451 --> 00:12:11,034
[SPEAKER_00]: Displeasure is manifested and you're liable to future manifestation of displeasure.

128
00:12:11,434 --> 00:12:18,778
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the truth that you will never understand, because all your actions revolve around avoiding that very truth.

129
00:12:21,615 --> 00:12:24,617
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, lying to other people is just extension of this very principle.

130
00:12:27,779 --> 00:12:34,304
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't undo that principle, as in lying to yourself, unless you stop habitually lying to other people.

131
00:12:35,405 --> 00:12:41,189
[SPEAKER_00]: But stopping to... stopping... ceasing to lie to other people doesn't necessarily mean you will automatically undo lying to yourself.

132
00:12:41,229 --> 00:12:42,270
[SPEAKER_00]: That takes a further effort.

133
00:12:42,590 --> 00:12:47,473
[SPEAKER_00]: But that further effort is only possible on the basis of the first effort of stop lying to others.

134
00:12:48,094 --> 00:12:50,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop proliferate... stop maintaining, basically,

135
00:12:53,044 --> 00:13:06,150
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop proliferating that wrong order of things through your acts of lying to others, so you reduce it a bit, and then you will be able to stop lying to yourself as well.

136
00:13:07,070 --> 00:13:08,851
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you can accept responsibility.

137
00:13:12,013 --> 00:13:14,754
[SPEAKER_00]: And suffering, as I said, is a fundamental responsibility.

138
00:13:14,774 --> 00:13:17,535
[SPEAKER_00]: See, you can say, oh, I suffer because he said this.

139
00:13:18,430 --> 00:13:22,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Or I suffer because I didn't get enough, or I suffer because, you know, I lost my family or something like that.

140
00:13:25,153 --> 00:13:34,236
[SPEAKER_00]: And if those things were truly rooted in... sorry, if suffering was truly rooted in those things, yeah, you wouldn't be able to be free from it.

141
00:13:35,937 --> 00:13:42,159
[SPEAKER_00]: But as the Buddha himself understood and then told to others, it... fortunately, it isn't rooted in those things.

142
00:13:43,797 --> 00:13:48,398
[SPEAKER_00]: It's rooted in your resistance of the disagreeable things that come your way.

143
00:13:49,118 --> 00:13:50,219
[SPEAKER_00]: But those are two different things.

144
00:13:50,659 --> 00:14:00,481
[SPEAKER_00]: But for as long as your actions maintain the view that it's in the things, in what he said, or she said, or what I had, what I lost, you will never take responsibility for suffering.

145
00:14:00,561 --> 00:14:05,423
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you will never take responsibility for your resistance to the disagreeable experience.

146
00:14:07,303 --> 00:14:10,604
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, you will always remain affected by the disagreeable experience.

147
00:14:11,580 --> 00:14:18,486
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the only way to stop resisting disagreeable experience is to take responsibility for the disagreeable experience.

148
00:14:19,067 --> 00:14:19,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Not lie to yourself.

149
00:14:20,068 --> 00:14:20,848
[SPEAKER_00]: Not lie to yourself.

150
00:14:21,028 --> 00:14:22,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Consequently, you won't be lying to others.

151
00:14:22,750 --> 00:14:23,871
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Arhat cannot lie.

152
00:14:24,752 --> 00:14:28,896
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because he became incapable of lying to himself where the problem is, because he solved the problem.

153
00:14:30,757 --> 00:14:32,359
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, the key is don't lie to yourself.

154
00:14:32,639 --> 00:14:32,839
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

155
00:14:33,520 --> 00:14:38,124
[SPEAKER_00]: But in order to not lie to yourself, you have to stop habitually lying to others.

156
00:14:44,990 --> 00:14:47,832
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what about these common white lines?

157
00:14:49,013 --> 00:14:49,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, again, like...

158
00:14:49,834 --> 00:14:51,255
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't look so bad.

159
00:14:51,335 --> 00:15:07,568
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not as bad, but you have to be strict and you have to see, does it partake in the principle of lying, principle you're trying to undo, principle of perverting the order of existence, putting the blame where the blame isn't.

160
00:15:09,032 --> 00:15:11,754
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you look at the white lies and stuff, yeah, it's still perverting.

161
00:15:12,014 --> 00:15:13,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would you white lying?

162
00:15:13,435 --> 00:15:14,795
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't want discomfort.

163
00:15:15,396 --> 00:15:16,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Why you don't want discomfort?

164
00:15:16,776 --> 00:15:19,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're blaming discomfort for pain.

165
00:15:19,658 --> 00:15:24,741
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, by not wanting discomfort, you're avoiding the responsibility for resisting the discomfort.

166
00:15:26,582 --> 00:15:28,523
[SPEAKER_00]: And blaming the discomfort for your suffering.

167
00:15:29,364 --> 00:15:32,906
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'll just white lie quickly, so I don't get discomfort means I won't suffer.

168
00:15:32,946 --> 00:15:33,066
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

169
00:15:33,486 --> 00:15:34,987
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't suffer when you don't resist.

170
00:15:35,692 --> 00:15:37,893
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that you don't suffer when there is no discomfort.

171
00:15:38,033 --> 00:15:39,994
[SPEAKER_00]: Arahant has discomfort, he has no suffering.

172
00:15:47,336 --> 00:15:48,657
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why white lies are bad.

173
00:15:55,159 --> 00:16:02,022
[SPEAKER_01]: And to... and if you... if you could save somebody from suffering, future...

174
00:16:02,770 --> 00:16:07,153
[SPEAKER_01]: By lying... Where is this person hiding?

175
00:16:07,173 --> 00:16:08,574
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, these people are looking for this.

176
00:16:08,834 --> 00:16:09,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right, right, right.

177
00:16:10,035 --> 00:16:18,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, fundamentally, you're always... On the most fundamental level, reasons for your action are always selfish.

178
00:16:18,341 --> 00:16:19,141
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot be otherwise.

179
00:16:20,062 --> 00:16:22,704
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you choose to help others, because that's what's going to make you happy.

180
00:16:23,665 --> 00:16:28,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you choose to help others, because that's how you're going to avoid personal discomfort.

181
00:16:30,836 --> 00:16:32,257
[SPEAKER_00]: So, others are always secondary.

182
00:16:32,837 --> 00:16:33,437
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not going...

183
00:16:33,537 --> 00:16:35,618
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way to go about this, is to accept it.

184
00:16:38,019 --> 00:16:43,502
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, stop perverting the order, as we... as we just spoke about.

185
00:16:44,082 --> 00:16:46,423
[SPEAKER_00]: So, stop blaming the circumstances for your suffering.

186
00:16:47,984 --> 00:16:52,766
[SPEAKER_00]: But, recognize that it's in you resisting the circumstances, that's where suffering is rooted.

187
00:16:54,647 --> 00:16:59,589
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when, like, you're hiding a fugitive or something,

188
00:17:01,397 --> 00:17:04,998
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you lie to others, like, no, I don't know where that person is.

189
00:17:05,658 --> 00:17:24,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you're saving their life, maybe, if that's at stake, I don't know, but what you're really acting out of is you being responsible for choosing to sort of have them in the first place and then, secondly, for not wanting to experience this comfort of responsibility.

190
00:17:25,902 --> 00:17:30,883
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you say the truth, they'll find that person and then things might happen to the person and you will be unable

191
00:17:31,299 --> 00:17:32,280
[SPEAKER_00]: To not blame yourself.

192
00:17:34,782 --> 00:17:36,564
[SPEAKER_00]: And blaming yourself, it's unpleasant.

193
00:17:36,584 --> 00:17:42,550
[SPEAKER_00]: So, really, you just want to prevent circumstances to allow you to engage in self-blaming.

194
00:17:43,731 --> 00:17:44,071
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

195
00:17:45,492 --> 00:17:46,473
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're doing it for yourself.

196
00:17:49,977 --> 00:17:51,818
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's what Buddha said in the suttas.

197
00:17:51,858 --> 00:17:56,703
[SPEAKER_00]: It's often mentioned, like, if a person who is truthful to himself, truthful to others,

198
00:17:57,141 --> 00:17:59,782
[SPEAKER_00]: If he's questioned by kings or authorities, have you seen this?

199
00:17:59,802 --> 00:18:00,942
[SPEAKER_00]: He will say, yes, I've seen it.

200
00:18:01,322 --> 00:18:03,642
[SPEAKER_00]: If he hasn't seen it, he'll say, no, I haven't seen it.

201
00:18:04,263 --> 00:18:06,503
[SPEAKER_00]: He won't say one or the other for no one's sake.

202
00:18:07,183 --> 00:18:08,063
[SPEAKER_00]: He'll say the truth.

203
00:18:09,464 --> 00:18:09,644
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

204
00:18:09,684 --> 00:18:11,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you say the truth.

205
00:18:11,524 --> 00:18:16,925
[SPEAKER_00]: And if these people that are chasing that fugitive choose to do certain things with that fugitive, that's not on you.

206
00:18:18,426 --> 00:18:20,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you say, yes, he went into that house.

207
00:18:21,346 --> 00:18:22,086
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not on you.

208
00:18:23,907 --> 00:18:25,087
[SPEAKER_00]: What they choose to do with him.

209
00:18:27,138 --> 00:18:28,439
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, why or to what extent?

210
00:18:28,579 --> 00:18:29,379
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not on you.

211
00:18:30,320 --> 00:18:33,641
[SPEAKER_00]: On you is to not pervert the existential order from your point of view.

212
00:18:35,002 --> 00:18:39,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Because by doing so, you're giving priority to your point of view, which means it's a slippery slope.

213
00:18:41,485 --> 00:18:54,471
[SPEAKER_00]: You act out of avoiding discomfort, means you're going to be acting out of prolonging pleasure, which means you're going to be acting out of conceit, out of delusion, out of... And that, those are the reasons for your own suffering, not the circumstances you've been subjected to.

214
00:18:55,778 --> 00:19:05,166
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, practically, through trying to avoid suffering, you end up making yourself more and more liable to suffering on that existential level.

215
00:19:08,629 --> 00:19:11,632
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, no lying, no stealing, no cheating, no killing.

216
00:19:12,853 --> 00:19:13,594
[SPEAKER_00]: Not negotiable.

217
00:19:17,577 --> 00:19:21,681
[SPEAKER_00]: Because without that basis, you cannot overcome

218
00:19:22,080 --> 00:19:25,942
[SPEAKER_00]: the attitude of avoiding responsibility for your own existence and for your own suffering.

219
00:19:26,022 --> 00:19:27,403
[SPEAKER_00]: You just can't, even if you want to.

220
00:19:27,683 --> 00:19:36,787
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a major offense for a monk if he lies about, you know, having attained a superior state.

221
00:19:37,507 --> 00:19:38,588
[SPEAKER_01]: He lies about his wisdom.

222
00:19:40,269 --> 00:19:41,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that, you know?

223
00:19:42,049 --> 00:19:43,290
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because the consequences of it.

224
00:19:45,357 --> 00:19:47,517
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the results of your action, that's why.

225
00:19:47,818 --> 00:19:49,938
[SPEAKER_01]: People take what you say as truth.

226
00:19:50,198 --> 00:19:51,798
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

227
00:19:51,958 --> 00:19:53,439
[SPEAKER_00]: And it can spread for generations.

228
00:19:54,099 --> 00:19:59,440
[SPEAKER_00]: And people will then be acting out of that as the truth, while in reality it isn't the truth.

229
00:20:00,580 --> 00:20:14,583
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, as the Buddha described in some other suttas, you know, like giving to an enlightened being, making offering to like somebody who is factually awakened and free from suffering, is the greatest merit.

230
00:20:16,191 --> 00:20:31,857
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if somebody is factually, or there is a word that such and such is factually free from suffering, he's an arahant, oh, I'll quickly go and make some merit, because giving to that person is going to have much more merit than giving to just any anybody else who isn't enlightened.

231
00:20:33,518 --> 00:20:41,521
[SPEAKER_00]: If that's the truth, you will actually get all those results, that great merit, because the person is factually an arahant.

232
00:20:42,341 --> 00:20:44,722
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's say somebody spreads a lie that he's an arahant.

233
00:20:46,121 --> 00:20:47,202
[SPEAKER_00]: And nobody knows it's a lie.

234
00:20:47,682 --> 00:20:53,825
[SPEAKER_00]: And then people go out of their way, and they don't give to others, because they want to give to this arahant.

235
00:20:55,706 --> 00:20:57,267
[SPEAKER_00]: And in reality, he isn't an arahant.

236
00:20:59,989 --> 00:21:04,391
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the whole mess created was because somebody lied that that person is an arahant.

237
00:21:05,071 --> 00:21:11,655
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as a direct result of that lie, millions have been misguided.

238
00:21:11,675 --> 00:21:13,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, many people have been misguided.

239
00:21:13,456 --> 00:21:15,397
[SPEAKER_00]: And they've been acting as if that person is an arahant,

240
00:21:16,918 --> 00:21:21,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Being sure they're going to get all this merit, dedicating all the offerings to that one person, because he's not... and it turns out he isn't.

241
00:21:24,623 --> 00:21:37,312
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they've been deceived directly as a result of that lie, which means the person who lied is going to be responsible for that mass deception cause, which can, as I said, can last for generations.

242
00:21:37,392 --> 00:21:38,393
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's bad.

243
00:21:39,114 --> 00:21:42,276
[SPEAKER_01]: You say that a monk could be deceiving himself.

244
00:21:43,428 --> 00:21:44,509
[SPEAKER_01]: Which then deceives others.

245
00:21:44,649 --> 00:21:45,510
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but that's different.

246
00:21:45,610 --> 00:21:51,254
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, like, that's why it's not an offense, if a monk does that out of overestimation.

247
00:21:51,274 --> 00:21:52,715
[SPEAKER_01]: He thinks, I know the way out.

248
00:21:52,735 --> 00:22:02,582
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I meant when I said, like, lying to others, it's not quite on the same level as lying to yourself, because lying to yourself means obscuring the truth.

249
00:22:02,762 --> 00:22:06,325
[SPEAKER_00]: So, simply through obscuring the truth, you're lying to yourself.

250
00:22:06,965 --> 00:22:11,789
[SPEAKER_00]: But when you come to lie to others, means you're fully aware of what truth is being obscured.

251
00:22:12,544 --> 00:22:14,365
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you act completely the opposite way.

252
00:22:14,785 --> 00:22:15,846
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's an extra layer.

253
00:22:17,706 --> 00:22:24,750
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the attitude of bad faith, avoiding responsibility, it's not like fully fledged as lying to another person.

254
00:22:24,770 --> 00:22:27,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Because in order to lie, you need to know what the truth is.

255
00:22:28,392 --> 00:22:31,113
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't lie if you think what you're saying is the truth.

256
00:22:31,353 --> 00:22:31,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

257
00:22:33,034 --> 00:22:35,495
[SPEAKER_00]: But when it comes to yourself, it's not that clear-cut.

258
00:22:36,455 --> 00:22:39,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're already refusing to admit what the truth is.

259
00:22:40,517 --> 00:22:43,798
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're refusing to be fully aware of what the truth is.

260
00:22:46,798 --> 00:22:49,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, you can't, like, fully lie to yourself.

261
00:22:49,399 --> 00:22:53,059
[SPEAKER_00]: Because in order to fully lie to yourself, you need to fully admit the truth to yourself.

262
00:22:53,680 --> 00:23:01,461
[SPEAKER_00]: But lying to yourself, it's not on the level of actually lying to yourself, but it's on a level of repressing or pressing, like turning away, ignoring the truth.

263
00:23:02,221 --> 00:23:03,661
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Vijaya is ignorance.

264
00:23:05,322 --> 00:23:06,222
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like a...

265
00:23:06,770 --> 00:23:09,632
[SPEAKER_00]: metaphysical lack of information of what the universe is.

266
00:23:10,213 --> 00:23:12,535
[SPEAKER_00]: It's ignoring what's right in front of you.

267
00:23:13,576 --> 00:23:17,118
[SPEAKER_00]: And lying to others comes on the on the basis of that principle.

268
00:23:17,259 --> 00:23:18,820
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just further proliferated.

269
00:23:19,180 --> 00:23:24,584
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's the sustaining of the thing that you... Oh, yeah, yeah.

270
00:23:25,205 --> 00:23:30,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Someone said, quote someone, lying is the lifeblood of addiction.

271
00:23:32,631 --> 00:23:33,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure.

272
00:23:33,351 --> 00:23:34,032
[SPEAKER_00]: But you could say that

273
00:23:36,118 --> 00:23:43,101
[SPEAKER_00]: Lifeblood of lying is avoiding self... is avoiding responsibility for your own experience.

274
00:23:44,961 --> 00:23:55,005
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, the basis for lying is... is attitude of bad faith to yourself, as in having faith in ignoring, not in what you're actually ignoring.

275
00:23:57,706 --> 00:23:58,826
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean, you pervert the order.

276
00:23:58,846 --> 00:24:00,907
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way you can ignore something is because it's there.

277
00:24:03,704 --> 00:24:10,847
[SPEAKER_00]: But you focus on prioritizing the act of ignoring, not the act of what's already there.

278
00:24:12,348 --> 00:24:19,792
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the most fundamental attitude towards the experience, the avijja, turning a blind eye.

279
00:24:19,812 --> 00:24:22,813
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way you can turn a blind eye to something is if that thing is already there.

280
00:24:25,554 --> 00:24:28,996
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the most fundamental attitudes of turning a blind eye

281
00:24:33,037 --> 00:24:36,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that fundamental wrong view in regard to the nature of existence?

282
00:24:38,061 --> 00:24:40,043
[SPEAKER_00]: Being...

283
00:24:40,083 --> 00:24:45,447
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not my own, as in, I'm not in control of this, of this body upon which my whole life depends.

284
00:24:46,207 --> 00:24:50,571
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm subject to aging, I'm subject to death, suffering.

285
00:24:51,111 --> 00:24:52,312
[SPEAKER_00]: All these things are obvious.

286
00:24:54,714 --> 00:24:59,898
[SPEAKER_00]: But the only reason people do not resolve them is because they give priority to ignoring them.

287
00:25:02,095 --> 00:25:02,695
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what I mean.

288
00:25:02,756 --> 00:25:08,400
[SPEAKER_00]: You give priority to your point of view on account of what's there in regard to which you exercise your point of view.

289
00:25:11,402 --> 00:25:15,946
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why ignoring the ignorance, it's like a vicious circle.

290
00:25:16,266 --> 00:25:17,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Avijja leads to more avijja.

291
00:25:18,468 --> 00:25:21,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignoring leads to maintenance of the attitude of ignoring.

292
00:25:23,132 --> 00:25:28,376
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what do I need to stop ignoring then, to undo the entire avijja of the whole saṃsāra?

293
00:25:29,637 --> 00:25:31,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop ignoring the arisen feelings.

294
00:25:31,770 --> 00:25:40,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop ignoring the reasoned intentions.

295
00:25:42,215 --> 00:25:53,721
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why person who has made that effort and stopped ignoring these things will become incapable of engaging in ill-will, anger, lying, cheating, even sensuality.

296
00:25:54,422 --> 00:25:59,204
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sensuality in itself, it's already that perversion on the fundamental level.

297
00:25:59,760 --> 00:26:02,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Finding pleasurable in that which is truly ugly.

298
00:26:03,402 --> 00:26:06,003
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you ignore that it's ugly.

299
00:26:08,564 --> 00:26:11,046
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignorance of the ugly is the basis for attraction.

300
00:26:11,506 --> 00:26:14,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignoring the ugly.

301
00:26:14,648 --> 00:26:15,308
[SPEAKER_01]: The unattractive.

302
00:26:15,988 --> 00:26:16,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

303
00:26:16,288 --> 00:26:17,449
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignoring the unattractiveness.

304
00:26:17,469 --> 00:26:18,850
[SPEAKER_00]: The fundamental unattractiveness.

305
00:26:19,790 --> 00:26:25,313
[SPEAKER_00]: The fundamental kind of dispassion.

306
00:26:26,878 --> 00:26:28,179
[SPEAKER_00]: Or absence of passion in it.

307
00:26:28,559 --> 00:26:32,320
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignoring that is how you have maintained passion in it.

308
00:26:33,941 --> 00:26:35,341
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the nature of concealment?

309
00:26:36,362 --> 00:26:50,548
[SPEAKER_00]: I suppose, eh... Well, in this... in the suttas, and often, you know, in many of them, in the Buddhist writings, you often hear about delusion, greed, aversion, delusion.

310
00:26:52,728 --> 00:26:53,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Lova, dosa and moha.

311
00:26:56,010 --> 00:26:56,310
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

312
00:26:59,103 --> 00:27:12,592
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, people can get their head around greed, can get their head around aversion, but often delusion is kind of regarded as something that's not directly my responsibility, inasmuch as greed is, or aversion.

313
00:27:13,072 --> 00:27:14,093
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know what I don't know.

314
00:27:14,874 --> 00:27:15,134
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

315
00:27:15,354 --> 00:27:23,560
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's regarded in a sort of a semi-metaphysical sense, as in, it's there, but it's not really something I can directly experience.

316
00:27:26,399 --> 00:27:27,920
[SPEAKER_00]: But actually, it is.

317
00:27:27,940 --> 00:27:37,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Delusion is an attitude of your mind in as much greed or lust and aversion are.

318
00:27:38,769 --> 00:27:43,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, you wouldn't have been mentioned always like that.

319
00:27:43,273 --> 00:27:43,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

320
00:27:44,694 --> 00:27:54,202
[SPEAKER_00]: So, from that point of view, delusion, it's more of like an attitude of indolence, laziness,

321
00:27:58,256 --> 00:27:59,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Choice to keep things cloudy.

322
00:28:00,737 --> 00:28:01,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

323
00:28:01,398 --> 00:28:03,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Keeping it opaque intentionally.

324
00:28:03,879 --> 00:28:05,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Not look... turning a blind eye.

325
00:28:06,420 --> 00:28:10,242
[SPEAKER_00]: Distracting yourself intentionally with things that are going to take your mind away from yourself.

326
00:28:11,903 --> 00:28:14,485
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize delusion is a very active thing.

327
00:28:15,625 --> 00:28:18,247
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as the Buddha said, that's why it's the most blameworthy, actually.

328
00:28:18,367 --> 00:28:20,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's the hardest to undo as well.

329
00:28:21,789 --> 00:28:26,251
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact that you can undo it, means it's not this metaphysical lack of knowledge.

330
00:28:27,372 --> 00:28:28,333
[SPEAKER_00]: It's your very attitude.

331
00:28:30,194 --> 00:28:33,976
[SPEAKER_00]: That it's even more fundamental than the attitude of lust and the attitude of aversion.

332
00:28:33,996 --> 00:28:35,176
[SPEAKER_01]: They're kind of secondary to it.

333
00:28:37,998 --> 00:28:40,319
[SPEAKER_00]: Lust requires a basis of delusion.

334
00:28:41,520 --> 00:28:43,621
[SPEAKER_00]: Aversion requires a basis of delusion.

335
00:28:44,802 --> 00:28:46,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Delusion requires a basis of delusion.

336
00:28:49,224 --> 00:28:55,168
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're deluded through and through, not because you don't know certain things, but because you perpetually act

337
00:28:55,893 --> 00:29:00,898
[SPEAKER_00]: Out of ignorance, that then maintains yourself in the situation of ignorance.

338
00:29:00,918 --> 00:29:04,701
[SPEAKER_00]: And situation of ignorance, again, it's not ignorance-ignorance, as in you don't know something.

339
00:29:05,161 --> 00:29:08,584
[SPEAKER_00]: It's you actively ignoring and giving priority to the ignoring of it.

340
00:29:13,249 --> 00:29:17,412
[SPEAKER_00]: So, choosing is... choosing to distract yourself, for example.

341
00:29:18,693 --> 00:29:22,557
[SPEAKER_00]: So, actively, actively making a choice,

342
00:29:23,587 --> 00:29:24,928
[SPEAKER_00]: They will result in certain actions.

343
00:29:25,608 --> 00:29:34,412
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not necessarily lustful or hateful, but are based upon, you know, wanting to be self-aware, for example.

344
00:29:36,293 --> 00:29:41,335
[SPEAKER_00]: So, entertainment or distraction in general.

345
00:29:41,395 --> 00:29:44,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'm bored by myself, because I'm aware of everything.

346
00:29:44,557 --> 00:29:45,277
[SPEAKER_00]: It's very unpleasant.

347
00:29:45,977 --> 00:29:47,938
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I just go and chat to some people, randomly.

348
00:29:48,018 --> 00:29:52,080
[SPEAKER_00]: Not for sensual reasons or because I hate them, just because I want something to do.

349
00:29:53,600 --> 00:30:12,088
[SPEAKER_00]: So, often, needing things to do is rooted in delusion, as in distraction, as in indolence, on the level of... Like, you can be very diligent externally, he's doing all this work, but the reasons for you doing that work are rooted in you wanting to turn a blind eye in regard to yourself.

350
00:30:12,609 --> 00:30:14,169
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to be self-aware, basically.

351
00:30:14,189 --> 00:30:16,210
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to see the truth of your mind.

352
00:30:16,971 --> 00:30:22,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, because that will result in the unpleasant truths of your mind, or simply unpleasant states of boredom and so on.

353
00:30:23,381 --> 00:30:24,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I better find something to do.

354
00:30:24,622 --> 00:30:28,104
[SPEAKER_00]: I better find an external purpose.

355
00:30:28,184 --> 00:30:33,767
[SPEAKER_00]: It will occupy my mind, so my mind won't be aware of itself, because being aware of itself is too unpleasant.

356
00:30:34,148 --> 00:30:35,809
[SPEAKER_00]: It's too threatening.

357
00:30:35,989 --> 00:30:37,790
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a way of keep covering up the truth.

358
00:30:38,610 --> 00:30:39,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

359
00:30:39,831 --> 00:30:45,055
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the way of maintaining yourself in a state of cover-up.

360
00:30:45,635 --> 00:30:46,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Keep the dust moving.

361
00:30:46,916 --> 00:30:47,696
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

362
00:30:47,776 --> 00:30:50,678
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, if it settles, you get to see what's there and it's unpleasant.

363
00:30:51,407 --> 00:30:52,327
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the thing.

364
00:30:52,387 --> 00:30:53,448
[SPEAKER_01]: You say self-aware.

365
00:30:54,008 --> 00:30:54,768
[SPEAKER_01]: It sounds like a good thing.

366
00:30:55,368 --> 00:30:57,949
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's like the most painful thing.

367
00:30:58,069 --> 00:30:59,670
[SPEAKER_01]: It is, yeah.

368
00:30:59,750 --> 00:31:01,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Things most people avoid.

369
00:31:01,610 --> 00:31:04,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's why solitude is very unpleasant to begin with.

370
00:31:05,672 --> 00:31:12,154
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, as the Buddha himself said many times, he practices so you develop the pleasure of solitude.

371
00:31:12,354 --> 00:31:13,414
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't get it right away.

372
00:31:13,595 --> 00:31:14,895
[SPEAKER_00]: If done rightly, it's unpleasant.

373
00:31:16,666 --> 00:31:22,050
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're going against the against the grain of all the distraction you got used to it and that your existence depends upon.

374
00:31:24,071 --> 00:31:24,531
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's it.

375
00:31:24,831 --> 00:31:38,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, when you get into like... when you stop maintaining all these attitudes of... they were based upon obscuring the self-awareness.

376
00:31:39,321 --> 00:31:42,603
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you stop feeding those attitudes, the self-awareness increases.

377
00:31:43,724 --> 00:31:44,765
[SPEAKER_00]: But it feels like

378
00:31:46,388 --> 00:31:50,092
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, the boredom starts giving rise to dread and anxiety.

379
00:31:50,132 --> 00:31:52,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Feels like you yourself are drowning.

380
00:31:52,634 --> 00:31:53,315
[SPEAKER_00]: It's crushing you.

381
00:31:54,396 --> 00:31:59,760
[SPEAKER_00]: But what is actually crushing is your assumed level of your own being, the one that you've been proliferating.

382
00:32:03,243 --> 00:32:04,685
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not actually crushing you.

383
00:32:06,066 --> 00:32:12,732
[SPEAKER_00]: Like being alone in your room, not seeing or talking to anyone, cannot do anything to you.

384
00:32:13,622 --> 00:32:16,123
[SPEAKER_00]: But yeah, your mind can sort of drown in it.

385
00:32:17,243 --> 00:32:22,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the mind became dependent upon a certain degree of engagement with the world.

386
00:32:22,945 --> 00:32:24,026
[SPEAKER_00]: That is its threshold.

387
00:32:25,046 --> 00:32:26,987
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not a static thing, that threshold.

388
00:32:27,647 --> 00:32:30,688
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, the more you engage, the more dependent upon engagement you become.

389
00:32:31,989 --> 00:32:41,272
[SPEAKER_00]: The less engaged, well, initially, it might hurt the lesser engagement, because you used to engage more, but then you get used to that.

390
00:32:41,872 --> 00:32:43,153
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it will not be unpleasant.

391
00:32:44,217 --> 00:33:01,202
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, like you have a very hectic person, who's been sort of doing a lot, engaging a lot, working a lot, for whatever reasons, and then, if he was made to sit down and stop engaging to that level, the discrepancy would be just too much to handle.

392
00:33:02,483 --> 00:33:12,046
[SPEAKER_00]: But if he gradually starts doing that, and then starts ceasing his engagement, he'll get used to that new level of lacking the engagement that he used to have.

393
00:33:12,938 --> 00:33:18,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, you are responsible for the threshold of where your being is.

394
00:33:18,600 --> 00:33:23,442
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when it says, his consciousness gets established upon that.

395
00:33:23,462 --> 00:33:26,103
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, that becomes the norm, that becomes what you're used to it.

396
00:33:26,123 --> 00:33:29,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, anything less than that, it's gonna fill you with anxiety and dread.

397
00:33:34,807 --> 00:33:37,188
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's important to recognize that it's not a static thing.

398
00:33:39,429 --> 00:33:42,030
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through... through using engagement,

399
00:33:43,768 --> 00:33:49,689
[SPEAKER_00]: With the world, actions, meaningless work, whatever else, or not even finding meaning for your work, whatever.

400
00:33:50,249 --> 00:33:57,391
[SPEAKER_00]: Practically, you're engaging with the world for the means of escaping self-awareness.

401
00:34:01,072 --> 00:34:05,573
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you will become dependent on that level of engagement.

402
00:34:07,273 --> 00:34:10,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything less than that engagement will bring you back to your self-awareness.

403
00:34:12,918 --> 00:34:22,484
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that means, then, you have to keep maintaining the level of your engagement and, once you get used to it, you have to keep increasing it, if you try to use it to avoid self-awareness.

404
00:34:23,224 --> 00:34:24,865
[SPEAKER_00]: And most people, to a degree, are using that.

405
00:34:24,885 --> 00:34:26,626
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why most people are afraid to be alone.

406
00:34:27,507 --> 00:34:38,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Not afraid from, like, some, you know, monsters and stuff, but afraid just, in broad daylight, being left alone in their room for a whole day, without doing anything, without taking my mind away from myself.

407
00:34:39,814 --> 00:34:41,935
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why most people would be afraid to be left alone.

408
00:34:43,333 --> 00:34:47,356
[SPEAKER_00]: It would result in experiencing anxiety and existential dread.

409
00:34:49,597 --> 00:34:57,462
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the human life, on average, is about avoiding that self-awareness, which ties in with avoiding irresponsibility and feeds one another and so on.

410
00:34:59,084 --> 00:35:02,866
[SPEAKER_01]: But that self-awareness is the thing that's going to make things right.

411
00:35:03,507 --> 00:35:03,947
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

412
00:35:04,187 --> 00:35:10,031
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people are, because of their own actions, they're liable to suffering.

413
00:35:10,211 --> 00:35:10,711
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that.

414
00:35:10,811 --> 00:35:11,652
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because of some

415
00:35:12,106 --> 00:35:14,387
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not someone else's fault or some design.

416
00:35:15,787 --> 00:35:26,110
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because you act in a manner that keeps making you liable to suffer, keeps you exposed.

417
00:35:26,130 --> 00:35:27,010
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you could then...

418
00:35:29,011 --> 00:35:33,713
[SPEAKER_00]: If you take responsibility for it, that's where what I'm saying might become apparent.

419
00:35:35,273 --> 00:35:41,875
[SPEAKER_00]: That the level of engagement, the level of distraction, the level of turning a blind eye, it's not a static thing.

420
00:35:42,318 --> 00:35:42,998
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you can...

421
00:35:43,299 --> 00:35:47,881
[SPEAKER_00]: The way you've been kind of proliferating it, you can then also undo it, if you stop engaging with it.

422
00:35:50,062 --> 00:35:52,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you can certainly put a brakes on its growth.

423
00:35:55,745 --> 00:35:59,307
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the bhāva in the suttas being... Again, it's not a metaphysical thing.

424
00:35:59,727 --> 00:36:03,849
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the level of your dependence on the senses, which is central being.

425
00:36:05,570 --> 00:36:10,813
[SPEAKER_00]: The level of dependence on the sense of the world, the duty,

426
00:36:11,141 --> 00:36:14,163
[SPEAKER_00]: The perception thing you perceive around the level you depend on it.

427
00:36:15,645 --> 00:36:17,546
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you can start depending less.

428
00:36:18,987 --> 00:36:21,910
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Arahant has cessation of his being.

429
00:36:22,570 --> 00:36:23,811
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't depend on anything.

430
00:36:25,272 --> 00:36:26,173
[SPEAKER_00]: But he's still there.

431
00:36:27,214 --> 00:36:28,995
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that the being means destruction.

432
00:36:29,976 --> 00:36:30,096
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

433
00:36:30,156 --> 00:36:31,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Being means assumption.

434
00:36:33,679 --> 00:36:38,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Assumption of engagement, assumption of avoiding responsibility, assumption of sensuality.

435
00:36:41,094 --> 00:36:43,775
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what delusion is.

436
00:36:44,176 --> 00:36:49,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignoring, turning a blind eye, distracting yourself, not being able to live with yourself.

437
00:36:50,739 --> 00:36:53,980
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, you can ask back onto that practical level that we always do.

438
00:36:54,681 --> 00:36:58,022
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it that I don't want to be self-aware?

439
00:36:59,003 --> 00:37:01,384
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it that I need to distract myself from myself?

440
00:37:02,865 --> 00:37:03,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's unpleasant.

441
00:37:06,066 --> 00:37:07,087
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why am I then

442
00:37:07,490 --> 00:37:14,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Maintaining this whole level of being for which I'm responsible, level of how much I depend on engagement with things.

443
00:37:14,871 --> 00:37:16,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's because I don't want to suffer.

444
00:37:19,372 --> 00:37:22,153
[SPEAKER_01]: But failing to see that my very engagement is...

445
00:37:22,233 --> 00:37:23,653
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the root of your suffering.

446
00:37:23,693 --> 00:37:24,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

447
00:37:24,213 --> 00:37:24,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

448
00:37:25,733 --> 00:37:26,933
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why do I suffer then?

449
00:37:27,454 --> 00:37:29,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's because I keep running away from suffering.

450
00:37:29,474 --> 00:37:32,374
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I suffer.

451
00:37:32,394 --> 00:37:35,735
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I stop running away from suffering, I at least have a chance to not suffer.

452
00:37:42,178 --> 00:37:48,661
[SPEAKER_01]: There was an example I heard, that people in prison...

453
00:37:48,841 --> 00:37:51,462
[SPEAKER_01]: In prison, punishment is solitary confinement.

454
00:37:51,582 --> 00:37:59,985
[SPEAKER_01]: And a lot of people would rather spend their time with other criminals, rapists or murderers, than be alone.

455
00:38:00,505 --> 00:38:00,785
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

456
00:38:02,546 --> 00:38:05,767
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's not accidental that, yeah, that was always the worst punishment.

457
00:38:05,807 --> 00:38:07,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Solitary confinement for prolonged periods of time.

458
00:38:09,722 --> 00:38:10,983
[SPEAKER_00]: For an untrained mind, of course.

459
00:38:11,023 --> 00:38:13,964
[SPEAKER_01]: You would rather do things that are bad for you.

460
00:38:14,465 --> 00:38:14,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

461
00:38:15,385 --> 00:38:20,948
[SPEAKER_00]: You would rather expose yourself to, like, genuine existential risks, than be alone with yourself.

462
00:38:21,188 --> 00:38:22,149
[SPEAKER_01]: I jump out of a plane.

463
00:38:22,749 --> 00:38:24,170
[SPEAKER_01]: I take a parachuting.

464
00:38:24,570 --> 00:38:25,791
[SPEAKER_01]: I put my life on the line.

465
00:38:26,952 --> 00:38:28,893
[SPEAKER_01]: I've got to do all these extreme sports, maybe.

466
00:38:29,373 --> 00:38:29,693
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

467
00:38:31,654 --> 00:38:33,335
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, just to avoid... Just to avoid, yeah.

468
00:38:34,436 --> 00:38:34,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's for me.

469
00:38:34,976 --> 00:38:38,138
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a stationary thing, which means...

470
00:38:39,478 --> 00:38:44,560
[SPEAKER_00]: Avoiding boredom needs to be maintained.

471
00:38:44,780 --> 00:38:47,441
[SPEAKER_00]: Boredom sets in when you get used to the threshold.

472
00:38:47,941 --> 00:38:50,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you need to do more.

473
00:38:50,842 --> 00:38:51,822
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with sensuality.

474
00:38:52,882 --> 00:39:00,144
[SPEAKER_00]: If people use sensuality as means of escape, if people get angry as means of escape, that's why you keep getting angrier and angrier as the life goes by.

475
00:39:01,893 --> 00:39:04,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you keep getting used to it, and it ceases to work.

476
00:39:04,714 --> 00:39:07,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Your means of escape cease to work, so then you need to keep running harder.

477
00:39:08,035 --> 00:39:09,035
[SPEAKER_01]: The truth is always there.

478
00:39:09,055 --> 00:39:11,096
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always kind of pushing up against you.

479
00:39:11,116 --> 00:39:18,638
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the fact, yeah, the fact that you give in to the attitude of ignoring the truth that's always there, means you're just running around in a circle.

480
00:39:19,898 --> 00:39:22,879
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't really ever run away from... And everybody knows that.

481
00:39:22,939 --> 00:39:27,320
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you ask any random person, even not familiar with Buddhism,

482
00:39:28,047 --> 00:39:29,868
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you satisfy your sensual desires?

483
00:39:30,188 --> 00:39:32,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Most people already know you can't, really.

484
00:39:34,371 --> 00:39:35,331
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't satisfy them.

485
00:39:35,351 --> 00:39:40,114
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only run around them.

486
00:39:40,134 --> 00:39:40,994
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with anger.

487
00:39:41,094 --> 00:39:49,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Is the satisfaction of revenge or inflicting pain back onto somebody, is that really preventing you from experiencing in the first place?

488
00:39:50,099 --> 00:39:50,299
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

489
00:39:50,379 --> 00:39:51,000
[SPEAKER_00]: You know that.

490
00:39:51,140 --> 00:39:52,040
[SPEAKER_00]: You just can't help it.

491
00:39:52,941 --> 00:39:54,902
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't help it because you keep ignoring the truth.

492
00:39:55,845 --> 00:39:58,127
[SPEAKER_00]: You keep ignoring the nature of what's right in front of you.

493
00:40:00,008 --> 00:40:07,734
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, ignoring will automatically, inevitably, result in lust and aversion, in greed and anger and so on.

494
00:40:13,238 --> 00:40:22,885
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't mean that, you know, if you lock yourself in the room and stop engaging with the world, that you will automatically arrive at the right understanding either.

495
00:40:26,075 --> 00:40:27,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's a sad thing.

496
00:40:28,196 --> 00:40:30,096
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha's instruction was necessary.

497
00:40:31,076 --> 00:40:33,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Or the instruction of somebody externally who has done it themselves right away.

498
00:40:40,959 --> 00:40:55,243
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the two outcomes for somebody who hasn't... who doesn't have that external instruction, the two outcomes would be... Yeah, the only two outcomes basically would be possible, if they were to, say, lock themselves in.

499
00:40:56,419 --> 00:41:13,450
[SPEAKER_00]: Either madness, as in the mind, would implode, because you wouldn't know the way out of itself, or you would develop a degree of that self-transparency and undo a great level of your being, which means become more transparent, less subjected to suffering, but you have to give in to something.

500
00:41:15,832 --> 00:41:23,337
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't be able to, like, remain... maintain that transparency all the way to the core of your being, whereby you've undone every single

501
00:41:24,249 --> 00:41:29,893
[SPEAKER_00]: slightest of existential discrepancies, or slightest of the conceits, as the Buddha called them, and become an arahant.

502
00:41:30,013 --> 00:41:32,095
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you do that, you would be, like, self-enlightened.

503
00:41:32,916 --> 00:41:36,298
[SPEAKER_00]: So, theoretically, it's possible, but practically, it's highly unlikely.

504
00:41:42,183 --> 00:41:43,884
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't mean that you shouldn't even try.

505
00:41:45,425 --> 00:41:53,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, even... even a person who is not necessarily interested in becoming fully enlightened and following the Buddha's instruction to the end, he would do it in good.

506
00:41:53,777 --> 00:42:04,402
[SPEAKER_00]: If they start withdrawing the level, or keeping in check the level of their engagement and dependence on the world and the senses... Why?

507
00:42:04,442 --> 00:42:22,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the life will end in you losing that dependence, through sickness, aging and death, losing your senses, losing the perception of the world, losing the things upon which your existence depends, or rather, things you used to maintain the level of being, that sort of help you to ignore

508
00:42:23,920 --> 00:42:39,369
[SPEAKER_00]: If the life were truly to last forever, and you will always stay in the control of your senses, yeah, that's fine.

509
00:42:39,530 --> 00:42:39,890
[SPEAKER_00]: No problem.

510
00:42:40,010 --> 00:42:40,450
[SPEAKER_00]: You can do it.

511
00:42:40,570 --> 00:42:41,030
[SPEAKER_00]: But it isn't.

512
00:42:41,131 --> 00:42:41,631
[SPEAKER_00]: And you know that.

513
00:42:41,711 --> 00:42:42,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody knows that.

514
00:42:43,252 --> 00:42:45,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, you act like you will live forever.

515
00:42:48,227 --> 00:42:55,031
[SPEAKER_00]: And the more you're dependent on the senses, they will disappear and break apart, the more you'll be affected, when that happens.

516
00:42:56,071 --> 00:42:59,453
[SPEAKER_00]: The less you're dependent, the less you'll be affected, means less you'll suffer.

517
00:43:00,774 --> 00:43:10,579
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like the simile Buddha gave, climbing the tree to eat the mangoes, and eventually, somebody is bound to come down, come up, and cut that tree, because they can't climb the tree and eat the mangoes.

518
00:43:11,099 --> 00:43:14,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Or even if they don't, the mangoes will age and break down, and the tree will fall.

519
00:43:15,482 --> 00:43:17,823
[SPEAKER_00]: The higher you were climbed on that tree,

520
00:43:18,203 --> 00:43:22,466
[SPEAKER_00]: The harder you will crash, the more suffering you will experience.

521
00:43:23,948 --> 00:43:35,797
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you kept yourself in check and stay maybe with lower branches, if you couldn't help yourself but climb the tree in the first place, then at least kept yourself in check, use your reason, self-reflection to not proliferate too far out.

522
00:43:37,819 --> 00:43:47,106
[SPEAKER_00]: In practical terms, you kept yourself in check how much you depend on distraction, how much you depend on other people, other people's company, how much you depend on gratuitous health,

523
00:43:47,667 --> 00:43:52,428
[SPEAKER_00]: Senses, ability to access the world and so on and distract yourself with it.

524
00:43:53,568 --> 00:43:58,949
[SPEAKER_00]: If you reflected on it, you will keep yourself in check and then don't go too... won't go too far in it.

525
00:44:00,250 --> 00:44:08,411
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, when you do get sick, when your senses do start to fail, when people leave you or die or don't do what you want to do, you won't be as affected.

526
00:44:09,171 --> 00:44:11,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you haven't made yourself as dependent.

527
00:44:23,247 --> 00:44:36,656
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you are, you know, you have concealed so much your whole life... Well, if you concealed so much your whole life, it's never too late to start undoing that concealment.

528
00:44:38,017 --> 00:44:39,418
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's really the problem there.

529
00:44:39,618 --> 00:44:52,547
[SPEAKER_00]: If you never start undoing your own... concealing basically yourself from yourself, concealing the nature of yourself from yourself... You've never actually faced displeasure.

530
00:44:53,594 --> 00:44:54,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

531
00:44:54,494 --> 00:44:57,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Never admitted it where it is, where the problem of it is.

532
00:44:57,636 --> 00:45:00,257
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people are responsible for being bound to saṃsāra.

533
00:45:00,617 --> 00:45:02,058
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not saṃsāra that binds you.

534
00:45:02,578 --> 00:45:03,879
[SPEAKER_00]: You bind yourself to it.

535
00:45:06,240 --> 00:45:16,284
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha said something to the same effect, when he said it's because people take what's not theirs, what shouldn't be theirs, what cannot be theirs, as if it's theirs and they act out of it.

536
00:45:17,685 --> 00:45:22,007
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Mahārāja does with them what he wants, controls them, subjects you to all these things.

537
00:45:22,697 --> 00:45:29,560
[SPEAKER_00]: What are the things that cannot be yours and shouldn't be yours and you keep taking as yours and that actually belong to mother, don't belong to you?

538
00:45:29,660 --> 00:45:32,921
[SPEAKER_00]: It's your eyes and your sights, your nose and your smells.

539
00:45:33,361 --> 00:45:37,322
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your very senses, the nature of them shows you that it's not yours.

540
00:45:37,402 --> 00:45:39,543
[SPEAKER_00]: You ignore it, you automatically take it as yours.

541
00:45:40,264 --> 00:45:46,046
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why just turning a blind eye is enough in itself to result in the whole mass of suffering.

542
00:45:46,706 --> 00:45:52,368
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like I turn a blind eye, then I do a second step of taking it as mine, then I do this, then...

543
00:45:52,772 --> 00:46:01,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Turning a blind eye to the nature of your senses, for example, your eyes, your nose, your body, your life, means automatically, implies taking it as mine.

544
00:46:02,574 --> 00:46:08,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if I ignore that it's not mine, I automatically imply that it's mine, that it's for me, that it's in my control.

545
00:46:09,716 --> 00:46:13,857
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you keep acting as if it is your control, and that's how you perpetuate turning of the blind eye.

546
00:46:14,377 --> 00:46:15,337
[SPEAKER_00]: You keep acting out of it.

547
00:46:16,157 --> 00:46:18,598
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to stop acting out of it, and then stop doing it.

548
00:46:19,491 --> 00:46:23,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Internally, stop turning that blind eye, start admitting it, go against the grain.

549
00:46:26,695 --> 00:46:32,138
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why everybody can benefit from solitude, to the extent that they are able to to practice it.

550
00:46:34,700 --> 00:46:39,963
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you don't practice it to fullest end, well, you have to take responsibility for that as well.

551
00:46:41,604 --> 00:46:46,407
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might find yourself already too far in life, whereby you can't, like, just

552
00:46:48,777 --> 00:46:55,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Withdraw from everything, because there will be consequences, but that's no one else's responsibility.

553
00:46:55,979 --> 00:47:01,341
[SPEAKER_00]: You are responsible for finding yourself in such situation.

554
00:47:03,442 --> 00:47:10,744
[SPEAKER_00]: But now people realize that that situation, they can't withdraw themselves from it, so then they feel justified in avoiding responsibility for it.

555
00:47:10,985 --> 00:47:12,185
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's completely extra.

556
00:47:13,147 --> 00:47:20,089
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you might not be able... you have infants and whatever, other family members depending on you, so you can't just leave because they'll die.

557
00:47:21,429 --> 00:47:27,051
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't mean you're justified now in ignoring responsibility for that situation that you yourself built up.

558
00:47:30,272 --> 00:47:38,094
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, you can't ignore the fact that your choices brought you in these circumstances that now other lives depend upon you.

559
00:47:40,363 --> 00:47:47,710
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't mean you should take responsibility for it, no matter how late that responsibility comes, the recognition that responsibility comes.

560
00:47:47,730 --> 00:47:50,873
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still better to take it, than to then ignore it further.

561
00:47:52,734 --> 00:47:53,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Because who knows?

562
00:47:53,615 --> 00:47:59,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Through taking responsibility, some new ways and options might present themselves, that you won't see for as long as you're avoiding responsibility for it.

563
00:48:01,883 --> 00:48:04,325
[SPEAKER_00]: But people don't take responsibility, because it's too unpleasant.

564
00:48:11,127 --> 00:48:15,309
[SPEAKER_01]: But yeah, taking responsibility, things become very clear, options become more...

565
00:48:15,850 --> 00:48:25,375
[SPEAKER_00]: But, because you're too concerned about not experiencing discomfort, you basically obscure and limit all these options that you could have otherwise became aware of.

566
00:48:25,595 --> 00:48:25,835
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

567
00:48:29,017 --> 00:48:36,821
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you actually get more resilience, less subjected to suffering, through recognizing, through admitting, through taking responsibility.

568
00:48:37,808 --> 00:48:41,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Initially, it's more unpleasant, but in the long run, it's actually far more pleasant.

569
00:48:42,610 --> 00:48:44,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Far less unpleasant, let's put it like that.

570
00:48:49,733 --> 00:48:52,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you're taking responsibility, you see where the problem is.

571
00:48:52,654 --> 00:48:54,875
[SPEAKER_00]: And the problem is in avoiding responsibility.

572
00:48:55,055 --> 00:48:55,835
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you suffer.

573
00:49:02,218 --> 00:49:06,079
[SPEAKER_00]: So you suffer, not because a disagreeable feeling touches you,

574
00:49:08,299 --> 00:49:13,504
[SPEAKER_00]: You suffer because you avoid responsibility for the suffering in regard to the disagreeable feeling.

575
00:49:15,185 --> 00:49:25,635
[SPEAKER_00]: You start taking responsibility for that suffering in regard to disagreeable feeling, you stop blaming disagreeable feeling for that suffering, which means you stop resisting it, which means you stop suffering.

576
00:49:27,637 --> 00:49:27,957
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

577
00:49:31,657 --> 00:49:32,458
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop touching it.

578
00:49:33,240 --> 00:49:34,282
[SPEAKER_00]: You stop touching it.

579
00:49:34,302 --> 00:49:38,168
[SPEAKER_00]: You stop getting into the domain that doesn't belong to you, that belongs to matter.

580
00:49:38,489 --> 00:49:39,811
[SPEAKER_00]: You stop taking it as yours.

581
00:49:40,512 --> 00:49:40,853
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

582
00:49:41,013 --> 00:49:42,616
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot be affected by anything then.

583
00:49:44,379 --> 00:49:45,381
[SPEAKER_00]: No matter how disagreeable.

